    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d8">

                <pb facs="0107" n="5" xml:id="pg5"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.51 War Credits/468½: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-04-19T12:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 19,
                                1927—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 2:40 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>24. Your telegram No. 17, April 18, 6 p.m. Minister of Foreign Affairs said that
                    Department’s views would be given prompt consideration by the Cabinet. He
                    appeared to be surprised and slightly embarrassed to learn that there had
                    already been offered in America the loan which the British Government had agreed
                    to support in return for the relinquishment by Greece of its claim for balance
                    of credits. Action will be slightly delayed by temporary Cabinet crisis arising
                    from resignation of Minister of Foreign Affairs, which he later withdrew, and by
                    Easter holidays which are about to begin. Is the Department sure that Speyer
                    &amp; Co. will abide by its wishes that pending the settlement of our debt no
                    Greek loan be floated in the United States?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
